elease build, BE-0402 config validation): (1) `SCENARIO-TABLE-DRIVEN MULTI-SHAPE OBSERVATION VERIFIER — ONE assess() FUNCTION FEEDS BOTH MEMBERS, RETURNS REASON STRINGS NOT t.Errorf CALLS` — the canonical pair shares an `assessRehearsalObservation(scen, status, err) []string` helper that takes a scenario and an (output, error) tuple and returns a list of human-readable failure reasons. The coverage member iterates the table in t.Run subtests and calls t.Errorf per reason inline; the burst member's worker goroutines call the same helper but record (worker, iteration, scenario, reasons) into a mutex-guarded slice that the main goroutine drains and reports after wg.Wait() — no testing.T calls from worker goroutines (race-detector hostile and stdlib-discouraged), same shape as BE-0395/BE-0396/BE-0397's burst patterns. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set table feeds both members AND the chokepoint surfaces a tuple (status, err) instead of a single verdict: a single string-returning assess() function is the source-of-truth predicate; iterate it in subtests for coverage, batch-collect-and-report for burst. (2) `CLOSED-SET ERROR-SHAPE ENUMERATION VIA TYPED KIND CONSTANT (Nil / Sentinel / Typed-yerr.Code / context.Canceled) RATHER THAN PER-SCENARIO ERROR LITERAL` — the rehearsalErrKind iota enumerates the four error shapes the reporter is documented to surface (rehearsalErrNil, rehearsalErrSentinelNoBackup, rehearsalErrTypedCodeServer, rehearsalErrContextCanceled). The scenario's wantErr field carries the kind, and assess() switches on the kind to choose the right errors.Is/errors.As predicate. Asserting on the kind (not on a literal error message) keeps the test stable across cosmetic message edits while still pinning the load-bearing typed-error contract. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint returns a documented closed-set error: don't hardcode literal messages — enumerate the kinds and switch on them in the verifier. (3) `SHARED REPORTER PER SCENARIO ACROSS BURST GOROUTINES — NOT FRESH BUILD PER GOROUTINE — TO PROVE THE DOCUMENTED CONCURRENT-SAFE CONTRACT` — backup.FileReporter is documented safe for concurrent use; building a fresh reporter per goroutine would not exercise that contract. The burst member builds one fixture per scenario UP FRONT (each fixture pairs the scenario with a built reporter), then every goroutine that draws scenario i shares the SAME reporter. A regression that introduced a shared mutable state (a memo cache, a non-snapshot read) in the reporter would surface as a goroutine seeing the WRONG scenario's predicate. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is documented safe for concurrent use: share the chokepoint instance across burst goroutines drawing the same scenario, never build fresh per goroutine — the burst's value is precisely the contention against the SAME instance.
